From 2720344c11423c0bab43a2cb2afdc158a87175ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:07:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 03/19] feat(core): validate multi-document manifests first;
 per-document results, dry_run and optional rollback (C28)

A multi-document apply that failed partway left the earlier documents
written (K8S-01; the fixed build now names them). On the native session
(job 13) an agent built four objects with four separate calls because
nothing could tell it in advance that one of them would be refused.

resources_create_or_update now:
- validates every document of a multi-document manifest with a
  server-side dry run (dryRun=All: admission, schema, RBAC, field
  ownership) before the first write. If any document is refused,
  nothing is written and the error lists every document with its
  verdict (ok / refused: reason). A document whose kind or namespace is
  created by an earlier document of the same input cannot be checked
  before that exists and is listed as "not checked" (checked at write
  time instead);
- takes `dry_run: true` to run only that validation, for one document
  or many, and return what the API would store;
- takes `on_failure: rollback` for a failure after the dry run (a
  quota, a webhook, a concurrent change): the objects this call
  created are deleted again in reverse order. This is a compensating
  action, not a transaction, and the result says so: it lists what was
  rolled back, what existed before and was changed and is NOT restored,
  and any delete that failed. The default, `on_failure: stop`, keeps
  the fixed build's behaviour (written documents stay and are listed).

Existence before each write is read only when a rollback is requested.

The fixed-build test for a refused second document now expects the
dry-run refusal with nothing written (the partial write it reproduced is
what this change prevents); the partial and rollback paths are tested
against a mock API server whose real write fails after the dry run
accepted it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
